1. Business Context
What is a "NetSite"?
A NetSite (Network Site) is a physical location — a rooftop, tower, utility pole, or ground parcel
— where Verizon Wireless installs wireless network equipment (antennas, radios, base
stations). Verizon does not own most of these locations outright; instead, it enters into lease
agreements with the property owners (landlords). Each NetSite therefore represents both:
- A physical location with geographic, parcel, and address attributes, and
- A legal and financial relationship between Verizon and the site owner, governed by a
contract.
Managing thousands of these leases — tracking their terms, payments, renewals, and
documents — is the core business problem this system solves.
What is VZW Fuze?
VZW Fuze is Verizon's internal platform for managing real estate and network infrastructure
operations. It is a suite of interconnected microservices covering everything from site acquisition
and contract management to payment processing and document storage.
fuze-netsites-service is the central microservice responsible for network site lease
contract management within that platform.
Who Uses This Service?
Role / Team What They Do
Real Estate Managers Create and manage lease contracts for new
and existing network sites
Finance / Accounts Payable Set up payment terms, process rent
payments, handle adjustments and refunds
Lease Administration Monitor contract renewals, execute options,
manage holdover situations
Quality Control (QC) Team Review and audit contract data for accuracy
and compliance
Legal / Compliance Manage contract documents, notice
recipients, and execution dates
IT / Platform Engineers Maintain data integrity, run bulk updates, and
refresh system caches
What Problem Does It Solve?
Without this service, Verizon would struggle to:
- Track thousands of active site leases and their exact payment obligations
- Automatically calculate rent adjustments tied to inflation (CPI)
- Ensure payments go to the correct payee accounts on the correct dates
- Maintain a complete, auditable history of every contract change
- Manage the document lifecycle for lease agreements in a central repository
This service provides a single, authoritative API for all of these operations, ensuring data
accuracy and process consistency across the entire Verizon network site portfolio.
2. Domain Glossary
Term Definition
Site A physical location where Verizon network
equipment is installed. Identified by a site ID.
Contract A legally binding lease agreement between
Verizon and a property owner for the use of a
specific site. Each contract has a unique
contract number.
Candidate A prospective site that has been identified for
network deployment but does not yet have a
fully executed lease contract. It is the first
stage before a contract is created.
Project A grouping of related site acquisition or
construction activities. A contract may be
associated with a project.
Parcel A legally defined piece of land (identified by a
parcel number / APN) associated with a site.
A single site may span multiple parcels.
Execution Date The date on which a lease contract was
formally signed and became legally binding.
Amendment A formal change to an existing contract —
e.g. extending the term, changing rent, or
adding new parcels.
Lock / Unlock Preventing (locking) or allowing (unlocking)
edits to a contract record. Contracts are
typically locked once payments are finalised
to prevent accidental changes.
Term Definition
Merge The process of combining or reconciling data
from an external system (HQRE) into the
NetSites database. A "merge" operation is an
upsert — insert if new, update if existing.
HQRE Headquarters Real Estate — Verizon's
authoritative source-of-truth system for
property and contract master data. Changes
in HQRE are periodically merged into
NetSites.
Payment Term The agreed-upon rent amount, payment
frequency (monthly, quarterly, annual), and
effective date range for a contract.
Commencement Date The date on which a payment term or a lease
term begins — i.e. when rent payments start.
Payee The entity (person or company) that receives
the rent payment. This may be the direct
landlord, a management company, or a
trustee.
Sublease Payee A secondary payee who receives a portion of
rent in a sublease arrangement, where the
primary tenant subleases part of the site.
Payment Schedule A generated list of individual payment due
dates and amounts derived from the payment
terms and commencement date.
Hold Payments A temporary suspension of rent payments for
a contract, usually due to a dispute, pending
execution, or legal hold. Tracked in hold
payment reference records.
Holdover A period in which a lease has technically
expired but Verizon continues to occupy the
site and make payments under the original
terms while a new agreement is negotiated.
Holdover logs track this status.
Term Definition
Special Payment A one-time or non-standard payment outside
the normal rent schedule — e.g. a lease
signing bonus, a co-location fee, or a
retroactive adjustment.
Abatement A temporary reduction or waiver of rent,
typically granted by the landlord as a
concession (e.g. during construction or as an
incentive to sign).
Payment Adjustment A correction applied to a past or future
payment to account for errors, CPI increases,
or renegotiated terms.
Finalize Payments The process of confirming and closing out the
payment schedule for a given period — after
which payments are sent to accounts payable
for disbursement.
Refund Payment A payment made back to Verizon by the
landlord — e.g. when overpayment is
discovered or a lease is terminated early.
Renewal Term A contractual clause that allows Verizon to
extend the lease for an additional period (e.g.
5 years) by exercising the renewal option.
Option Term A specific renewal period defined in the
contract, often with pre-negotiated rent rates.
Verizon must formally "exercise" the option
before a deadline to trigger it.
CPI (Consumer Price Index) A government measure of inflation published
by the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS).
Many leases include rent escalation clauses
tied to CPI changes.
LRC (Lease Rate Change) The calculated change in rent resulting from a
CPI escalation clause. LRC payment terms
define the new rent amount after applying the
CPI adjustment.
Term Definition
BLS Data Statistical data from the U.S. Bureau of Labor
Statistics used to compute CPI-based rent
adjustments. The system fetches series data
and area-specific CPI figures.
Audit Event A recorded entry that tracks who made a
change to a contract record, what changed,
and when. Provides a full, tamper-evident
change history.
QC Event A Quality Control review task assigned to a
team member to verify the accuracy of
contract data. QC events have a lifecycle:
created → assigned → evaluated → closed.
NetDocs Verizon's internal document management
system (built on Documentum) where lease
agreements, amendments, and related
documents are stored and versioned.
Documentum The enterprise content management platform
underlying NetDocs. Stores and versions
documents attached to contracts.
File Tracker A system that tracks the physical or electronic
status and location of document files as they
move through the signing and archiving
workflow.
Notice Recipient A person or entity that must legally receive
official notices (e.g. lease renewal notices,
termination notices) related to a contract, as
specified in the lease agreement.
Regional Manager A Verizon Real Estate manager responsible
for overseeing site contracts within a specific
geographic region.
Custom Attribute A flexible, user-defined data field that can be
attached to a contract or site record to
capture non-standard information specific to
that deal.
Term Definition
Master Contract A parent-level contract that governs multiple
individual site contracts — often used for
portfolio deals where one landlord owns
many sites.
Associated Contract A contract that is linked to another contract
(e.g. an amendment is associated with the
original lease).
Ownership Fields Data fields recording who owns the site
property — the legal owner, any affiliate
relationships, and any changes in ownership
over time.
DataCloud Verizon's internal framework for executing
named SQL queries stored in the database,
abstracting away raw JDBC complexity.
Bulk Update An operation that modifies the same field(s)
across many contracts simultaneously, rather
than editing them one by one. Requires
approval before execution.
ERP Enterprise Resource Planning — the financial
system (e.g. SAP) that ultimately processes
and disburses the rent payments generated
by this service.
GSAM A data classification for site, party, and
contract records referring to their association
with the GSAM (Global Site Asset
Management) framework used for portfolio
reporting.
3. Core Business Processes
3.1 Contract Lifecycle
Step 1 — Candidate Site Creation
Trigger: A network planning team identifies a location where a cell tower or small cell is
needed.
1. A Candidate record is created in the system (mergeNetsitesCandidate), capturing
the proposed site address, region, project association, and initial property ownership
details.
2. The candidate is linked to a Project (mergeNetsitesProject) that groups related site
work.
3. Custom attributes may be added to capture deal-specific notes
(mergeFUZECustomAttributes).
Outcome: A candidate record exists in the system representing a prospective site, ready for
lease negotiation.
Step 2 — Contract Creation
Trigger: Verizon negotiates and agrees on lease terms with the property owner.
1. A new Contract record is created (NetsitesContractCreation), capturing the
contract number, site ID, landlord/payee details, effective dates, and initial rent terms.
2. The contract is associated with the candidate site and project.
3. Payment Terms are set up (getPaymentTerms,
mergeNetsitesPaytermCommencement) defining rent amount, frequency, and start
date.
4. Payees are recorded (NetSitesPayees) specifying who receives payments and to
which account.
5. Notice Recipients are added (mergeNetSitesNoticeRecipient) for legal
notification obligations.
6. Parcels are linked (NetSitesInsertParcel) to record which land parcels are
covered.
7. Contract documents are uploaded to NetDocs (saveContractDocumentDetails).
Outcome: A fully configured contract record with payment terms, documents, and associated
parties.
Step 3 — Contract Amendment
Trigger: A change in lease terms is agreed (rent renegotiation, term extension, parcel addition,
etc.).
1. An Amendment record is created (NetsitesContractAmendment) linked to the
original contract.
2. Affected fields are updated — payment terms, parcels, option terms, custom attributes.
3. New documents reflecting the amendment are uploaded to NetDocs.
4. An Audit Event is recorded capturing what changed, by whom, and when.
Outcome: The contract record reflects the amended terms while preserving full history of all
prior versions.
Step 4 — Contract Execution
Trigger: The lease or amendment is fully signed by all parties.
1. The Execution Date is recorded on the contract (NetSitesContractExecResp).
2. The contract status is updated to "Executed."
3. The payment schedule is generated based on the commencement date and payment
terms.
4. The contract is Locked (ContractLock / MergeNetsitesLockUnlock) to prevent
inadvertent edits.
Outcome: An active, locked, executed contract with a live payment schedule.
Contract Locking and Unlocking
- Lock: Applied after execution or payment finalization. Prevents edits to critical fields.
Recorded in the lock/unlock audit log.
- Unlock: Requires authorisation. Applied when a correction or amendment must be
made to a locked contract. The contract is re-locked after the change.
- Both operations are tracked with the requesting user's identity and timestamp.
Contract Merging / Consolidation
Trigger: Bulk data synchronisation from the HQRE source-of-truth system, or manual
consolidation of duplicate records.
1. The HQRE system sends a batch of contract/site data payloads (List<HQREInfo>).
2. The mergeNetsitesContract operation performs an upsert — if a contract with that
ID already exists it is updated; otherwise a new one is created.
3. Associated data (custom attributes, notice recipients, parcels) is merged in the same
batch.
Outcome: The NetSites database stays in sync with the HQRE master data system.
3.2 Payment Management
Setting Up Payment Terms and Commencement Dates
1. After contract creation, a Payment Term record is created specifying: rent amount,
payment frequency (monthly / quarterly / annual), effective start and end dates, and the
payment method code (e.g. ACH, check).
2. The Commencement Date (mergeNetsitesPaytermCommencement) is set as the
date from which rent calculations begin.
3. If the lease has a LRC (Lease Rate Change) clause, LRC payment terms are
configured separately (LrcPaymentTerms, LrcTermCommencement).
Outcome: The system knows exactly when rent starts, how much is owed, and how often
payments must be made.
Payment Schedule Generation
1. The system uses the payment terms and commencement date to generate a list of
individual payment due dates and amounts.
2. The schedule is stored and viewable via GetNetsitesPaymentsFull /
getNetSitesPayments.
3. Schedules can be validated for conflicts before finalisation
(checkScheduleInvalidRequest).
Outcome: A complete, date-by-date rent payment schedule for the life of the contract.
Payment History Tracking
- All payments that have been made are recorded in the payment history
(GetNetsitesPaymentHist, GetNetsitesPaymentHistFull).
- Users can query full or summarised payment history for any contract.
- The history provides the information needed for reconciliation with the ERP / accounts
payable system.
Payment Adjustments and Special Payments
- Adjustments (GetNetSitesPaymentAdjustmentLog,
MergeNetSitesPaymentUpdates) correct amounts for specific payment periods —
e.g. when a retroactive CPI increase is applied.
- Special Payments (MergeNetSitesSpecialPmts, SpecialPmtsBreakdown)
handle one-off payments outside the regular schedule — signing bonuses, construction
fees, or settlement payments.
- All adjustments are logged with reason codes and user attribution.
Hold Payments & Holdover Logs
- Hold Payments (HoldPmtsSelectRequest, MergeNetsitesHoldPmtRef)
temporarily suspend scheduled payments — for example, when a lease is being
renegotiated or there is a legal dispute.
- Holdover Logs (GetNetsitesHoldOverLogs) track periods where the lease has
expired but the site is still occupied under holdover terms, so payments continue at a
potentially different rate.
Finalizing Payments / Refund Payments
- Finalize (NetsitesFinalizePmts): Confirms and closes the payment schedule for a
period, sending it downstream for ERP disbursement. Once finalized, the contract is
locked.
- Refund (MergeNetsitesRefundPayment): Records a payment made back to
Verizon, such as overpayment recovery or early termination credit.
3.3 Renewal & Option Terms
Adding and Managing Renewal Terms
1. During contract creation or amendment, Renewal Terms (NetSitesRenewalTerms)
are recorded — e.g. "Verizon may renew for three 5-year periods."
2. Each renewal term specifies the start date, duration, and rent amount or escalation
formula.
3. Terms are managed via mergeRenewalTerms, and can be deleted in bulk
(NetSitesRenewalTermsDeleteAll).
Option Terms Management
1. Option Terms (MergeNetSitesOptionTerms) are specific contractual renewal
election windows — dates by which Verizon must notify the landlord of its intent to
exercise a renewal.
2. When Verizon elects to renew, the option commencement date is set
(MergeNetSitesOptionTermsCommence), activating the next lease period.
3. Unneeded option terms can be deleted individually or in bulk
(NetSitesOptionTermsDeleteAll).
CPI Adjustments on Renewal
1. Many leases include a rent escalation clause tied to the Consumer Price Index (CPI).
2. The system fetches current CPI data from the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics
(BLSDataRequest / BLSDataResponse, area series data CpiAreaSeries,
CpiAreaData).
3. A CPI calculation (CPICalRequest, ContractCPIHistoryRequest) computes the
new rent amount by applying the percentage change in CPI to the current rent.
4. The resulting LRC (Lease Rate Change) payment terms are stored and a new payment
schedule is generated.
Outcome: Rent is automatically adjusted in line with inflation at each renewal, as required by
the lease.
3.4 Site & Parcel Management
Managing Site Parcels and Property Details
- Each site is linked to one or more Parcels — legally described pieces of land with APN
(Assessor's Parcel Number) identifiers.
- Parcels can be inserted (NetSitesInsertParcel), updated
(UpdateNetSitesParcels), or merged (MergeNetSitesParcels) as property
boundaries change.
- The GSAM records (NetSitesSiteGSAM, NetSitesPartyGSAM,
NetSitesContractGSAM) link sites to the broader portfolio management framework.
Temporary Equipment Tracking
- When construction or temporary installations are placed at a site, Temporary
Equipment records (NetSitesTempEquipment, GetNetSitesTempEquipment)
track what is on-site, when it was placed, and when it will be removed.
- This ensures billing for temporary equipment is separate from the permanent lease
obligations.
Address Details Lookup
- The system can validate and enrich a site address (getAddressDetails) using the
GetAddressProcedure — looking up geocoordinates, standardising the address
format, and verifying municipality/county data.
- Dropdown lookups for city (NetsitesGetDropdownCityRequest) and county
(NetsitesGetDropdownCountyRequest) ensure standardised address entry.
3.5 Document Management (NetDocs)
Uploading and Retrieving Contract Documents
1. Signed lease agreements, amendments, and supporting documents are uploaded
(documentUpload via DocumentServiceClient) and linked to a contract in
NetDocs.
2. Document metadata (file name, document type, upload date, uploader) is saved
alongside the file (saveDocumentDetails, saveContractDocumentDetails).
3. Documents can be retrieved by document ID (getDocumentGet) and presented to
users for review.
Documentum Audit Trail and History
- Every time a document is created, moved, revised, or viewed, NetDocs records the
event.
- The Audit Trail (NetDocsAuditTrail, getNetDocsAuditTable) shows who
accessed or modified each document and when.
- Document History (NetDocsHistory, getNetDocsHistoryTable) shows prior
versions of a document, enabling users to review what a contract looked like before an
amendment.
File Tracker Integration
- File Tracker (NetSitesFileTrackerRequest) monitors the physical/electronic
movement of contract files through the review and signing workflow — e.g. "sent to
landlord," "received signed," "archived."
- This provides end-to-end visibility into whether a document is still awaiting signature or
has been fully executed and stored.
3.6 Quality Control (QC) & Auditing
QC Audit Event Creation and Assignment
1. A QC Audit (MergeNetsitesQcAudit) is created when a contract record or payment
schedule requires formal review — triggered by a change, a regular audit cycle, or a
data discrepancy flag.
2. The audit is assigned (AuditAssignRequest, qcAuditAssignRequest) to a
specific QC analyst who is responsible for reviewing it.
Evaluating and Updating QC Events
1. The assigned analyst reviews the contract data, comparing it against source documents.
2. Findings are recorded as QC Events (MergeNetsitesQcEvent,
UpdateNetsitesQcEvent) — each event describes what was checked, the finding
(pass / fail / exception), and any corrective action taken.
3. Events can go through multiple update cycles (UpdateNetsitesQcEvent2,
UpdateNetsitesQcEventEval) as corrections are made and verified.
4. Once all issues are resolved, the QC audit is closed.
Audit Trail Review
- Every data change — contract updates, payment adjustments, document uploads, QC
actions — is recorded as an Audit Event with the user, timestamp, and before/after
values.
- This provides a complete, tamper-evident history required for legal and regulatory
compliance.
- Users can query the full audit history for any contract at any time.
3.7 Bulk Operations
Bulk Contract Updates
Trigger: A change needs to be applied to many contracts simultaneously — e.g. a
portfolio-wide rate change or a data correction affecting a class of sites.
1. An authorised user defines the fields to update (BulkUpdateFields) and the set of
contract numbers to target.
2. The bulk update record is inserted into a staging table (NetsitesInsBulkUpdTable)
for tracking.
3. An approval workflow is triggered (NetSitesBulkUpdateApproval) — a second
authorised user must review and approve the change before it is executed.
4. Upon approval, the BulkServiceClient calls the bulk-upload-service
(bulkContractNbrInsert) to apply the changes at scale.
5. The bulk update table (NetsitesGetBulkUpdTable) tracks the status of each record
in the batch.
Outcome: Hundreds or thousands of contracts are updated in a single controlled operation,
with full audit trail and approval gate.
Script Execution via Bulk Service
- For complex data migration scenarios, SQL scripts can be submitted for execution via
executeScripts (ScriptExecutionRequest / ScriptExecutionResponse).
- This is used by platform engineers for one-off data corrections that cannot be expressed
as a standard bulk update.
3.8 User & Access Management
User Login and Registration
- When a user first accesses the platform, a Login procedure (LoginProcedure)
validates their credentials against the Oracle user store.
- New users are registered via RegisterUserProcedure, creating a user profile linked
to their roles and privileges.
Fetching User Roles and Privileges
- The system enforces role-based access control. On every API request, the user header
(containing the logged-in user's identity) is validated by RequestFilter.
- User roles (getUserRolesRequest) and privileges (getUserPrivilegeRequest)
are loaded from the cache and checked before sensitive operations are allowed — e.g.
only Finance team members can finalise payments; only managers can approve bulk
updates.
Notice Recipient Management
- For each contract, the lease specifies which parties must receive formal legal notices
(e.g. renewal election notices, termination notices).
- Notice Recipients (NetSitesNoticeRecipient) are added, updated, or removed as
the contract lifecycle progresses (mergeNetSitesNoticeRecipient,
mergeNetSitesNoticeRecipientForAll).
- This ensures Verizon meets its legal notification obligations.
Regional Manager Assignment
- Contracts are assigned to a Regional Manager who is responsible for their oversight.
- Regional manager data (GetNetsitesRegionalManagers,
NetSitesRegionalManagerResp) allows querying which managers own which
contracts and facilitates workload distribution and reporting.
3.9 Data Refresh / Cache Management
What the Cache Holds
The CachedResultsDao loads and holds in memory:
- System-level configuration settings and feature flags
- User, role, and privilege reference data
- Lookup / reference tables (payment method codes, status codes, contract types)
- Any other frequently read, rarely changing data that would otherwise require a database
round-trip on every API call
Why it matters: The system handles hundreds of concurrent requests. Caching these lookup
tables eliminates thousands of repetitive database queries per minute, dramatically improving
response times.
When to Refresh the Cache
The cache is loaded automatically at startup by LoadDataCloudOnStartup. However, if an
administrator changes a system setting, role definition, or lookup table value in the database,
those changes will not be visible to the running service until the cache is refreshed.
The /fuze-netsites-service/datacloud/refresh endpoint triggers a manual refresh
— re-running all cache queries and reloading the DataCloud SQL registry — without requiring a
service restart.
When to call it: After any change to system configuration tables, reference data, or
the SQL query registry in Oracle.
4. Key Business Rules
1. Payments cannot be finalised on an unlocked contract. A contract must pass
through the lock workflow before the payment schedule is submitted to accounts
payable, preventing mid-stream edits to payment amounts.
2. CPI rent escalations use official BLS data. The system does not allow manual CPI
percentage input for escalation calculations — it must fetch the official index values from
the Bureau of Labor Statistics data feed, ensuring regulatory and audit compliance.
3. Every request must carry a valid user identity. The user HTTP header is mandatory
on all API calls. Requests without a valid, authenticated user are rejected with a 401
Unauthorized response before reaching any business logic.
4. Bulk updates require a two-step approval. No bulk contract modification can be
executed without a second authorised approver — preventing accidental or unauthorised
mass changes to the contract portfolio.
5. Merges are upserts — they never hard-delete. The merge operation from HQRE
always inserts or updates; it does not delete existing records. Data integrity is preserved
even if a record disappears from the source feed temporarily.
6. Notice recipients are contract-specific, not global. Each contract maintains its own
list of notice recipients; a change to one contract's recipient list does not affect any other
contract.
7. Contract locking is user-attributed and logged. Every lock and unlock action records
the user who performed it and the timestamp, creating an auditable trail of who had write
access and when.
8. Option terms must be exercised before a deadline. The system tracks option
exercise windows. If an option is not actioned before the contractual deadline, it lapses
and the renewal right is lost.
9. Holdover status continues payment obligations. A contract in holdover status
remains financially active — payments continue to be scheduled and processed — even
though the original lease term has expired.
10. Document uploads require a contract linkage. Documents saved via NetDocs must
be associated with a contract record (saveContractDocumentDetails); orphaned
documents are not permitted in the workflow.
11. CyberArk vault is used for database credentials in production. The database
password is never stored in plain text in production environments — it is retrieved at
runtime from a CyberArk secrets vault, controlled by the is_cyberark_enabled flag.
12. Custom attributes are flexible but audited. Contracts may carry any number of
user-defined custom attributes (mergeFUZECustomAttributes), but all attribute
changes are recorded in the audit history.
